 ('frz3', 'The Hoysalas are the ones to know for architecture. Their temples at Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura are built on star plans, on raised platforms, in soapstone — a stone soft enough to be worked almost like wood, and which hardens with exposure.'),
 ('frz4', 'The result is carving of extraordinary density: horizontal bands running right round the building, each with its own subject. Several of their sculptors signed their work, which is rare.'),
 ('frz5', 'The Kakatiyas matter for something less often taught and more useful: water. The Telangana plateau has no great river running through it, so the Kakatiyas and their chiefs built tanks — thousands of them, bunded reservoirs catching the monsoon and releasing it through sluices into fields below.'),
 ('frz6', "Inscriptions record their construction and their upkeep. It is one of the clearest cases in ancient India of a state's power resting on an engineering decision."),
 ('frz7', 'Two more things. Devagiri, the Yadava capital, is a fortress on an isolated hill with concentric defences, considered near-impregnable in its day.'),
 ('frz8', 'And across all four kingdoms the merchant guilds were powerful — the Ayyavole guild in particular appears in inscriptions across the Deccan and beyond, which tells you that long-distance trade carried on regardless of which dynasty held which capital.'),
